"Karlstad University (https://www.kau.se/files/2024-06/Book_of_Abstracts%20%281%29.pdf)\nciteturn4search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.4 years ago; We study the Minkowski dimension, also called the box dimension, of degenerate spiral trajectories of a class of ordinary differential equations. ... We compute the box dimension of a polynomial degenerate focus of type \\((n,n)\\) by exploiting the well-known fractal results for \\(\\alpha\\)-power spirals. In the general focus type \\((m,n)\\) case, we formulate a conjecture about the box dimension of a degenerate focus backed up with numerical experiments.\nKarlstad University\n\nof Zagreb, Zagreb, Croatia).\n\nWe study the Minkowski dimension, also called the box dimension, of degenerate spiral trajectories of a class of ordinary differential equations. Specifically, a class of singularities of focus type with two zero eigenvalues (nilpotent or more degenerate) has been studied. We compute the box dimension of a polynomial degenerate focus of type \\((n,n)\\) by exploiting the well-known fractal results for \\(\\alpha\\)-power spirals. In the general focus type \\((m,n)\\) case, we formulate a conjecture about the box dimension of a degenerate focus backed up with numerical experiments. Additionally, we construct a three-dimensional vector field that contains a degenerate spiral, called an elliptical power spiral, as a trajectory.\n\n[1] R. Huzak, D. Vlah, D. Žubrinić, V. Županović, *Fractal analysis of degenerate spiral trajectories of a class of ordinary differential equations* Appl. Math. Comput. **438** (2023); 127569.\n\n[CT15] ODE-systems with boundary conditions containing higher order derivatives\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFractal analysis of degenerate spiral trajectories of a class of ordinary differential equations | Document Server@UHasselt (https://documentserver.uhasselt.be/handle/1942/38722)\nciteturn4search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; Abstract:   | In this paper we initiate the study of the Minkowski dimension, also called the box dimension, of degenerate spiral trajectories of a class of ordinary differential equations. ... We find the box dimension of a polynomial degenerate focus of type (n, n ) by exploiting the well-known fractal results for α-power spirals. In the general (m, n ) case, we formulate a conjecture about the box dimension of a degenerate focus using numerical experiments.\n\nPlease use this identifier to cite or link to this item: `http://hdl.handle.net/1942/38722`\n\nTitle:   | Fractal analysis of degenerate spiral trajectories of a class of ordinary differential equations\nAuthors:   | HUZAK, Renato \nVlah, Domagoj\nZubrinic, Darko\nZupanovic, Vesna\nIssue Date:   | 2023\nPublisher:   | ELSEVIER SCIENCE INC\nSource:   | Applied mathematics and computation, 438 (Art N° 127569)\nAbstract:   | In this paper we initiate the study of the Minkowski dimension, also called the box dimension, of degenerate spiral trajectories of a class of ordinary differential equations. A class of singularities of focus type with two zero eigenvalues (nilpotent or more degenerate) has been studied. We find the box dimension of a polynomial degenerate focus of type (n, n ) by exploiting the well-known fractal results for α-power spirals. In the general (m, n ) case, we formulate a conjecture about the box dimension of a degenerate focus using numerical experiments. Further, we reduce the fractal analysis of planar nilpotent contact points to the study of the box dimension of a slow-fast spiral generated by their “entry-exit” function. There exists a bijective correspondence between the box dimension of the slow-fast spirals and the codimension of contact points. We also construct a three-dimensional vector field that contains a degenerate spiral, called an elliptical power spiral, as a trajectory.\nKeywords:   | Box dimension (Minkowski dimension);Degenerate spiral trajectories;Geometric chirps;Turning points\nDocument URI:   | http://hdl.handle.net/1942/38722\nISSN:   | 0096-3003\ne-ISSN:   | 1873-5649\nDOI:   | 10.1016/j.amc.2022.127569\nISI #:   | 000866507400001\nDatasets of the publication:   | https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0096300322006439?dgcid=coauthor\nRights:   | 2022 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.\nCategory:   | A1\nType:   | Journal Contribution\nValidations:   | ecoom 2024\nAppears in Collections:  | Research publications\n\n###### Files in This Item:\n\nFile  | Description  | Size  | Format  |  \n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nFractal_analysis_of_degenerate_spiral_trajectories_of_a_class_of_ordinary_differential_equations.pdf  | Non Peer-reviewed author version  | 582.59 kB  | Adobe PDF  | View/Open\n1-s2.0-S0096300322006439-main.pdf  |  | 922.08 kB  | Adobe PDF  | View/Open\nFractal analysis of degenerate spiral trajectories of a class of ordinary differential equations.pdf\n  Restricted Access  | Published version  | 703.43 kB  | Adobe PDF  | View/Open    Request a copy--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGeodesic complexity of the octahedron, and an algorithm for cut loci on convex polyhedra (https://pranavraj575.github.io/assets/pdf/geodesic_complexity.pdf)\nciteturn4search13 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 9 months ago; ** The geodesic complexity of a dodecahedron is at least four (needing at least five sets). ... Our motivation for believing Conjectures C.1 and C.2 is that the cut locus of the center of an icosahedron (resp. dodecahedron) face is a star with six (resp. ten) lines (Figure 26). ... Our motivation for Conjectures C.3 and C.4 is that the tetrahedron [5], cube [6], and now the octahedron have geodesic complexity at most four.\n• \\(x^{\\{2,4,5\\}}(p)=\\left(p_1\\frac{-p_1-\\sqrt{3}p_2+4\\sqrt{3}}{-p_1+\\sqrt{3}p_2+2\\sqrt{3}},\\,\\frac{-p_1p_2+2p_1-\\sqrt{3}p_2^2-2\\sqrt{3}p_2}{-p_1+\\sqrt{3}p_2+2\\sqrt{3}}\\right).\\)\n\n• \\(x^{\\{3,4,5\\}}(p)=\\left(2p_1\\fra